From 99dd0ba2a5eda45fc21a2dbead1fa4b023028137 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 16:01:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] lex/proc_macro: a byte literal stays a byte literal (`b'a'`,
 not `97u8`)

cssparser 0.35.0 (ani-cli-rs 0.11.0) tokenizer.rs:475, `skip_whitespace`:

    match_byte! { self.next_byte_unchecked(),
        b' ' | b'\t' => { self.advance(1) },
        b'\n' | b'\x0C' | b'\r' => { self.consume_newline(); },
        ..
    }

    thread 'main' panicked at :0:0:
    Found a pattern that wasn't a byte
    MACRO<::"cssparser_macros-0_6_1"::match_byte> BUG:
      src/expand/proc_macro.cpp:2449: Unexpected EOF while reading from
      child process

cssparser-macros 0.6.1 accepts only `syn::Lit::Byte` patterns. The lexer
made `b' '` the same token as `32u8` (TOK_INTEGER of type u8), so the
proc-macro bridge sent an integer, libproc_macro printed `32u8` and syn
parsed a `Lit::Int`.

- token.hpp/.cpp: the integer token carries `m_is_byte_char`
  (Token::make_byte_char, is_byte_char()); it is part of token equality
  (macro_rules tells `b'a'` from `97u8`, as rustc) and to_str() prints
  `b'..'` with rustc's escapes (stringify!, re-lexing).
- lex.cpp: `b'x'` makes one.
- serialise/deserialise: the flag is the top bit of the integer token's
  type tag, so a dependency's macro_rules body keeps it; older rlibs never
  set the bit and still load.
- expand/proc_macro.cpp: new bridge token class ByteChar (13) in both
  directions (TokenSink::send_byte_char; the TokenCollector builds the
  token too).
- lib/libproc_macro: protocol Token::ByteChar, LiteralValue::ByteChar
  printed as rustc does (`b'\x0c'`, `b'\''`), Literal::byte_character()
  makes one (it gave UnsignedInt(.., 8) = `97u8`).

The compiler and libproc_macro must be updated together (an older
libproc_macro panics on tag 13). Not covered: a byte literal that went
through the AST (an attribute macro's item, `$e:expr`) still reaches
a proc macro as `97u8`; same value and type.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
